<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Second Coalition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1802">1802</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Second Coalition was fought between Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and others. After early coalition successes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1799, including the battles around Zürich, France under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> won at Marengo and Hohenlinden in 1800. The treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a> in 1801 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> in 1802 ended the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-second-coalition.jpg" alt="War of the Second Coalition" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruedi33a · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_second_coalition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a> (1792–97), which ended with the Treaty of Leoben on 18 April 1797 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in October 1797, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to fight on alone. Peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was uneasy: the French demanded territory the treaty did not name, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> were reluctant to hand over what it did, and in 1798 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> angered Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-of-russia">Paul I of Russia</a>, the honorary head of the order of knights that ruled the island.</p><p>The coalition took months to form. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 19 May 1798 and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on 29 November, and Russia allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> on 23 December and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on 26 December. Prussia declined to join.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> aimed to stop the French Republic's expansion and restore the monarchy, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, weakened and deep in debt, sought chiefly to recover its position. In 1799 the Russian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Alexander Suvorov</a> won a series of victories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and drove the French back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. The Anglo-Russian invasion of Holland failed, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> an Austro-Russian army was routed at the Second Battle of Zurich. These reverses, and British searches of shipping in the Baltic, led Russia to leave the coalition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in October 1799 and a coup made him First Consul.</p><p>In 1800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> led an army across the Great St. Bernard Pass and narrowly defeated the Austrians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Battle of Marengo</a>, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> agreed to evacuate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. Moreau invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hohenlinden">Battle of Hohenlinden</a>, and the Armistice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steyr">Steyr</a> followed on 25 December. In 1801 the British navy attacked the Danish fleet at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, after the Second League of Armed Neutrality had formed against searches of neutral ships, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and British forced the French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to surrender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> signed the Armistice of Treviso on 16 January 1801 and, on 9 February, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> for the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, in effect accepting the terms of Campo Formio. France kept all its earlier gains and obtained new lands in Tuscany, while Austria was granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> and the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-dalmatia">Venetian Dalmatia</a>. Most other allies signed separate peaces in 1801. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and France signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a> in March 1802, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in June 1802. The peace lasted several months, until Britain declared war on France in May 1803 and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. The coalition had failed to overthrow the revolutionary government, largely because of differences of strategy among its three major powers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Second Coalition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1799 Zürich was a battlefield in the <b>War of the Second Coalition</b>, when French forces under André Masséna fought Austrian and then Russian armies in the two Battles of Zürich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Lieutenant Field Marshal Anton Sztáray, commanding the far right wing of the Austrian army in Swabia, defeated the French at Wiesloch in the <b>War of the Second Coalition</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-3">3 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
